  <section id="chto-eto">
    <h2>What it is</h2>
    <p>The internal carotid arteries are the two main routes carrying blood to the brain. <strong>Occlusion</strong> is complete blockage of the artery, most often by a plaque with a clot on it. Blood no longer flows through it.</p>
    <p>Yet the brain is not left without supply straight away. It has a safety net: the other carotid artery, two vertebral arteries and a network of vessels connecting them — <strong>collaterals</strong>. If they cope, a person may have no symptoms. If not, the brain is "starved", and sooner or later this ends in ischaemic attacks or a stroke.</p>
    <h3>Risk factors</h3>
    <p>The same as for carotid plaques: atherosclerosis, high cholesterol, high blood pressure, diabetes, smoking, age over 50. More about plaques — in the article <a href="/en/treatment/vascular/carotid-stenosis/">"Carotid artery stenosis"</a>.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="simptomy">
    <h2>Symptoms</h2>
    <p>Occlusion is often found after a stroke. But there may be warning signs — <strong>transient ischaemic attacks</strong>, episodes that pass on their own within minutes or hours:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>weakness or numbness of an arm, leg or half of the face;</li>
      <li>speech problems;</li>
      <li>brief loss of vision in one eye;</li>
      <li>dizziness and unsteadiness, especially on standing up.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Repeated attacks despite treatment are one of the main signs that collateral flow is not enough.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="diagnostika">
    <h2>Diagnosis</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Neck ultrasound</strong> — often the first test showing that there is no flow in the artery.</li>
      <li><strong>CT or MR angiography</strong> — confirms the occlusion and shows which vessels have taken over the load.</li>
      <li><strong>Brain MRI</strong> — shows whether there have been strokes, including silent ones.</li>
      <li><strong>CT perfusion</strong> — the key test for the decision. It shows how much blood each area of the brain actually gets, so the hemispheres can be compared.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section id="komu">
    <h2>Who needs surgery</h2>
    <p>Not everyone with a blocked artery needs surgery. I offer it only when three conditions are met together:</p>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>The occlusion is confirmed</strong> by imaging.</li>
      <li><strong>There are symptoms</strong> of insufficient blood supply: ischaemic attacks or a stroke, especially repeated despite treatment.</li>
      <li><strong>CT perfusion shows a deficit</strong> of blood supply on the side of the blocked artery.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>If even one condition is missing, medication chosen by a doctor and monitoring are usually enough. I explain these criteria <a href={`${tg}/197`} target="_blank" rel="noopener">in cards on my Telegram channel</a> (in Russian).</p>
    <div class="callout">
      <p>Revascularisation is also done for <a href="/en/treatment/vascular/moyamoya/">moyamoya disease</a> — there the brain arteries narrow for a different reason, but the task is the same: give the brain extra blood flow.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="operaciya">
    <h2>EC-IC bypass</h2>
    <p><strong>EC-IC bypass</strong> (extracranial-intracranial microanastomosis) is bypass surgery for brain vessels: a detour for blood around the blocked segment.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>A branch of the <strong>superficial temporal artery</strong>, which normally supplies the scalp, is dissected under the skin of the temple.</li>
      <li>Through a small window in the bone it is brought to an artery on the surface of the brain — usually a branch of the middle cerebral artery.</li>
      <li>Under the microscope the vessels are sewn together with the finest thread. Their diameter is about 1–2 mm.</li>
      <li>The bone is put back and fixed with titanium plates.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>This is not "cleaning" a vessel, as in endarterectomy, but creating a new route. Its goal is to <strong>reduce the risk of another stroke</strong>. Quality of life does not always improve after surgery, and I discuss this honestly with every patient before the decision.</p>
    <figure class="case-fig">
      <div class="pair2">
        <div><img src="/media/cases/bypass-before.webp" alt="CT angiography before EC-IC bypass" loading="lazy" width="900" height="900"><span>Before</span></div>
        <div><img src="/media/cases/bypass-after.webp" alt="CT angiography after EC-IC bypass: temporal artery sewn to a brain artery" loading="lazy" width="900" height="900"><span>After</span></div>
      </div>
      <figcaption>A case from my practice: after bypass a branch of the temporal artery enters the skull and supplies brain vessels.</figcaption>
