    <h3>Cases from my practice</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>Both internal carotid arteries are blocked, blood flows only through the vertebral arteries, and there has already been a stroke. <a href={`${tg}/185`} target="_blank" rel="noopener">Surgery video and follow-up</a> (in Russian).</li>
      <li>A man of about 40: a stroke, one side of the body failed. After EC-IC bypass the ischaemic attacks stopped — <a href={`${tg}/237`} target="_blank" rel="noopener">case review</a> and <a href={`${tg}/238`} target="_blank" rel="noopener">his feedback two months later</a> (in Russian).</li>
      <li>After a stroke with speech problems: at follow-up a few months later speech and memory are better and the bypass works — <a href={`${tg}/219`} target="_blank" rel="noopener">the patient’s story</a> is on my Telegram channel (in Russian).</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section id="posle">
    <h2>After surgery</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Drugs to prevent clots and treat atherosclerosis continue for life — the regimen is prescribed by a doctor. Surgery does not treat atherosclerosis itself.</li>
      <li>Bypass function is checked with ultrasound and CT angiography on the schedule set by the surgeon. What a follow-up looks like I showed <a href={`${tg}/77`} target="_blank" rel="noopener">on a 3D reconstruction</a> (in Russian).</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

